{
  "dom_id": "step-636927",
  "name": "Intuition Behind the Formula",
  "problem": "We've been using the following formula for the area of the region bounded by a polar curve ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/7031a0f3ab71df36bc36e925fcf4f04dbfc4feec5f374092855ef8d97b104452.svg) and the rays ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/38b971de9610efe2ed0de9f10956c73cf5cd967a86a5bcbb076791f369c22bef.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/818e2605a585628c4becd20664b58007dbd2348ca664c80be6a838868a64693a.svg)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/55ffcf934c3a9e33a3376fa1796e6fb68ad1198d719db5ea094fe8eb46c5f47d.svg)\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo see where the formula comes from, we can think about breaking up the area bounded by a polar curve into infinitesimally small sectors of circles.\n\nAs shown in the graph below, each sector has a radius of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) and spans an infinitesimally small angle, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/0904a69fa5e6a3a10eaf74b3fdd865db92761eeeffffae1c0badd33ffdf5b34b.svg)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/82be86e7092a97ca5b2f1423f32b15030af0c8c63bf47ece8be40b45720ac905.png)\n\nThe ratio of the area of a sector to the full area of the circle must be the same as the ratio of the angle of the sector to a full revolution of the circle. So, if the area of a sector is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/0b2376d17b173024d6f6c82faaff585293e9830ba7ce0764ecf8338adcd14994.svg) then we can set up and solve the following proportion:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/5d318385ab16ee14d784b33c00180a44ade7cee2e97ffeafd290171d0d44868f.svg)\n\nFinally, to find the area under the curve, we can integrate the areas of all the sectors:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217747/assets/a75940f74a02ceadff519af028783a0e704fba8a73c4abf36c6a371deb56dee7.svg)",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"step-636927\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block\" stepid=\"636927\" steptype=\"tutorial\" contentid=\"4582\">\n            <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                <div class=\"stepName\">\n                    <a id=\"stepAnchor-636927\" class=\"stepAnchor\" name=\"step-11\">Intuition Behind the Formula</a>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"636927\">?</div>\n            </div> \n\n            <p>We've been using the following formula for the area of the region bounded by a polar curve <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.879ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3482.6 1000\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><path data-c=\"1D45F\" d=\"M21 287Q22 290 23 295T28 317T38 348T53 381T73 411T99 433T132 442Q161 442 183 430T214 408T225 388Q227 382 228 382T236 389Q284 441 347 441H350Q398 441 422 400Q430 381 430 363Q430 333 417 315T391 292T366 288Q346 288 334 299T322 328Q322 376 378 392Q356 405 342 405Q286 405 239 331Q229 315 224 298T190 165Q156 25 151 16Q138 -11 108 -11Q95 -11 87 -5T76 7T74 17Q74 30 114 189T154 366Q154 405 128 405Q107 405 92 377T68 316T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(728.8,0)\"><path data-c=\"3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1784.6,0)\"><path data-c=\"1D45F\" d=\"M21 287Q22 290 23 295T28 317T38 348T53 381T73 411T99 433T132 442Q161 442 183 430T214 408T225 388Q227 382 228 382T236 389Q284 441 347 441H350Q398 441 422 400Q430 381 430 363Q430 333 417 315T391 292T366 288Q346 288 334 299T322 328Q322 376 378